# Operator connections

An Operator reacts when a bound Verifier run ends with a `failed` verdict. An
Operator Card never carries a secret. It names a **connection**: a credential
that belongs to one tenant, is stored encrypted in Postgres, and is looked up by
provider and name each time the Operator delivers.

This page covers creating, reading, rotating, and disabling connections, and
how each provider's Operator uses one.

## What a connection is

- **Tenant-bound.** A connection belongs to the tenant that created it. An
  Operator resolves only a connection in the tenant of the run that failed.
- **Typed.** The provider is one of `slack`, `pager_duty`, or `http`. Provider
  and name are fixed at creation, and the name is unique per provider within
  the tenant.
- **Identified by a UUIDv7.** The server mints `connection_id` at creation.
- **Encrypted at rest.** Each secret version is encrypted with its own data
  key. The data key is wrapped by a key-encryption key held outside Postgres
  (see [Operator connection keys](/self-hosting/configuration/#operator-connection-keys)).
  Postgres never holds a plaintext secret.
- **Write-only secret.** Reads return `connection_id`, `provider`, `name`,
  `status`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, and the provider's nonsecret settings
  only: `workspace_id` for Slack, nothing for PagerDuty, and the origin, auth
  scheme, and custom header name for HTTP. No operation returns a token, key,
  username, password, or header value.

## Manage connections

Every surface calls the same server operations. Reads need `operators:read`;
every change needs `operators:write`. Of the built-in roles only `admin` holds
them (see [Authorization](/concepts/authorization/)).

| Operation | HTTP | CLI |
|---|---|---|
| Create | `POST /v1/operator-connections` | `wyrd operator-connection create --body-file <path>` |
| List | `GET /v1/operator-connections` | `wyrd operator-connection list` |
| Get | `GET /v1/operator-connections/{connection_id}` | `wyrd operator-connection get --connection-id <uuid>` |
| Update, rotate, re-enable | `PATCH /v1/operator-connections/{connection_id}` | `wyrd operator-connection update --connection-id <uuid> --body-file <path>` |
| Disable | `DELETE /v1/operator-connections/{connection_id}` | `wyrd operator-connection disable --connection-id <uuid>` |

The MCP tools `operator_connections.list` and `operator_connections.get` are
always listed; `operator_connections.create`, `operator_connections.update`, and
`operator_connections.disable` appear only to a caller holding
`operators:write`. The Rust, Python, and TypeScript SDKs expose the same
operations. The CLI reads the request body from a file, or stdin with
`--body-file -`, so a secret never appears on the command line.

### Rotate a secret

Send a PATCH with the provider tag and the new secret. Omitted fields are kept;
a supplied secret replaces the stored one atomically. The next delivery attempt
uses the new secret. No Card revision or server restart is needed.

```yaml
provider: slack
bot_token: xoxb-new-token
```

### Disable and re-enable

DELETE disables a connection. The row is kept for Card, dispatch, and audit
lineage; it is never physically deleted. While disabled, registration and
delivery refuse it. Re-enable it with a PATCH:

```yaml
provider: pager_duty
status: active
```

If a connection is missing, disabled, of the wrong provider, or from another
tenant, registration and delivery fail closed with the same error, so the
response does not reveal which condition applied.

## Slack

A Slack connection holds one workspace bot token with the `chat:write` scope.

```yaml
provider: slack
name: ops-slack
workspace_id: T0123456789
bot_token: xoxb-...
```

A Slack Notify Operator names the connection and a conversation ID:

```yaml
kind: notify
channel:
  kind: slack
  connection: ops-slack
  channel_id: C0123456789
  text: "{{subject_ref}} failed {{verifier_ref}}"
```

Wyrd calls `chat.postMessage` with the bot token and treats a JSON `ok: false`
response as a failure, not only a non-2xx status. The Slack app must already be
allowed to post in that conversation. Wyrd does not need `chat:write.public`,
does not manage channel membership, and stores no per-channel webhook URL.

## PagerDuty

A PagerDuty connection holds one Events API v2 Global Integration key.

```yaml
provider: pager_duty
name: ops-pagerduty
integration_key: R0123456789ABCDEF...
```

A PagerDuty Notify Operator names the connection and a route:

```yaml
kind: notify
channel:
  kind: pager_duty
  connection: ops-pagerduty
  route: payments-oncall
  severity: critical
  summary: "{{subject_ref}} failed {{verifier_ref}}"
```

Wyrd sends one `trigger` event. The authored `route` is carried as
`payload.custom_details.wyrd_route`; match on it in PagerDuty Service Routes to
send the event to the right service. PagerDuty owns routing and escalation;
Wyrd does no escalation mapping and needs no per-team integration key. The
`dedup_key` is the rendered `dedup_key` template when you set one, otherwise
the dispatch ID, and stays the same across retries of one dispatch. An accepted
event does not prove that PagerDuty opened an incident or paged anyone.

## HTTP

An HTTP connection holds one credential for exactly one origin.

```yaml
provider: http
name: hooks-prod
origin: https://hooks.example.com
auth:
  scheme: header
  name: X-API-Key
  value: ...
```

- `origin` is `https://host[:port]` with no path, query, fragment, or userinfo.
  Plain `http` is accepted only for a loopback host.
- `auth` is `bearer { token }`, `basic { username, password }`, or
  `header { name, value }`. A custom header cannot be `Authorization`, `Host`,
  `Content-Length`, `Transfer-Encoding`, `Connection`, or `Idempotency-Key`.

An HTTP Operator names the connection under the same scheme, and header name
for `header`, compared case-insensitively:

```yaml
kind: http
method: post
url: https://hooks.example.com/wyrd/{{binding_id}}
auth:
  scheme: header
  name: X-API-Key
  connection: hooks-prod
body:
  run: "{{run_id}}"
  verdict: "{{verdict}}"
```

The URL origin is literal; placeholders may appear only in the path and query.
At registration and before every attempt, the auth scheme, header name, and
origin of every effective URL must match the connection, or the request fails
before the secret is decrypted. The destination address is screened and pinned
before the credential is attached, only same-origin redirects are followed, and
each request carries `Idempotency-Key: <dispatch_id>`. An unauthenticated HTTP
Operator omits `auth` and needs no connection.

## Delivery

Each failure Operator gets its own durable dispatch. A dispatch has three
attempts of up to 30 seconds each, with 30-second then two-minute backoff,
inside a five-minute deadline. Connection failures, timeouts, an unavailable
key store, 408, 429, and 5xx responses retry; missing or unauthorized
credentials, invalid destinations or templates, and other 4xx responses fail
terminally. Delivery is at least once, so a destination may see a dispatch more
than once. Dispatch status is readable from
`GET /v1/verification/runs/{run_id}`.

## What is not available

- **Workflow Operators.** A `workflow` Operator Card registers, but a binding
  that lists it under `on_failure` is rejected at registration. Wyrd never
  reports an uninvoked Workflow as delivered.
- **The old alert router.** The signed-webhook alert router and its
  configuration were removed. There is no `Alert` resource or second delivery
  path; a Notify Operator is the alert.
